  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Vertically stacked low pressure will meander off\nthe NC and Mid-Atlantic coast through mid week bringing\nprolonged strong north to northeasterly winds across ENC. The \nsfc low is approx 270 mi east of Cape Hatteras and will only \nvery slowly drift northward over the next few days as the system\nremains cut-off from the mean flow aloft. It is not until \nWednesday night or Thursday that the low will begin to \naccelerate away from the East Coast as a northern stream \nshortwave digs into the Mid-Atlantic and Northeast. \n\nThere remains some variability among the models with the \nplacement of the low as it drifts northward over the next couple\nof days, especially beyond Tuesday as some guidance now has the\nlow a bit farther northeast of the area allowing the winds to \ndiminish some. However, sufficient solutions keeps the low close\nenough to the coast that Gale Force conditions may persist \nalong the coast through mid week. \n\nTight pressure gradients between the offshore low and high\npressure across the Eastern CONUS will bring strong north to\nnortheast winds across the region with the strongest winds\nacross the Outer Banks where gusts up to 50 mph will be\npossible. A Wind Advisory remains in effect from Duck to \nOcracoke, and continues through Tuesday. \n\nThe strong winds continue to bring large surf along the coast \nas well as coastal flooding impacts along vulnerable locations \nof the coast, especially along Hatteras and Ocracoke Islands \nwhere dune structure is fragile. The National Parks Service and\nNCDOT have recently reported substantial beach erosion from\nsouth Nags Head to Buxton, along with significant overwash\noccurring on side streets in Rodanthe. Due to hazardous road\nconditions and flooding, NC-12 is now closed from the Marc\nBasnight Bridge to Rodanthe, and ferry service remains\nsuspended.\n\nEach subsequent high tide cycle will continue to threaten the \ndunes, further, and the threat of additional impacts to Hwy 12 \nremain. Soundside flooding is also a concern along southern \nportions of the Pamlico Sound, including Frisco, Ocracoke, areas\nof Downeast Carteret Co and locations along the Neuse and Bay \nRivers. For some areas, water levels may reach the highest now\nthat the wind direction has shifted to more of a north or even\nnorth-northwest direction (specifically for Ocracoke and\nHatteras Villages). A Coastal Flood Warning remains in effect \nfor Hatteras and Ocracoke Islands and a Coastal Flood Advisory \nremains in effect for the Northern Outer Banks and several \ncounties along the sounds and rivers where northerly winds will \nbring elevated water levels. Additionally, a high surf advisory \ncontinues for Cape Lookout and points north through the OBX. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Pleasantly cooler air mass that has advected in\nwill remain with us through at least mid week with temps a bit \nbelow climo. Maximum temperatures through the period are \nexpected to remain in the 60-70 degree range. Overnight lows \nwill range from the 40s inland to the 50s coast. \n\nAs the low meanders offshore, some wrap around rain showers may\noccasionally sneak back onshore, with best chances on the OBX \nover the next couple of days.",
